# msil-jitall.py runs jitall under a wall-clock timeout (plan M0.c): a JIT
# can hang on bad IL. Force-JITting <Module> runs its initializer, which here
# spins (Inputs/jitall-spin-cctor.il), so jitall must be killed after
# --timeout seconds and the output must end with '[timeout]'. The option
# comes after lit's default --timeout, so it wins. The initializer gives up
# by itself after 120 s, so the outer 60 s bound also catches a jitall that
# waits for it instead of killing it. On the WSL leg this is the timeout of
# the Linux side.
# REQUIRES: msil-jitall

# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t.dll %S/Inputs/jitall-spin-cctor.il
# RUN: not %{harness_check} within 60 %msil_jitall --timeout 5 %t.dll > %t.log 2>&1
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.log "^\[timeout\]$" "^harness-check: finished in"
